The `md_html` function passes `input` and `input_size` directly to `md_parse`. The input is not treated as a C string. So the "c-string format" in the description likely refers to the input containing null bytes (which is what makes it a "c-string" - it has embedded nulls).

Let me look at the `md_analyze_line` function's `strcspn` usage more carefully. The key issue is:

```c
if(ctx->doc_ends_with_newline  &&  off < ctx->size) {
    while(TRUE) {
        off += (OFF) strcspn(STR(off), "\r\n");

        if(CH(off) == _T('\0'))
            off++;
        else
            break;
    }
}
```

If the input ends with a newline, `doc_ends_with_newline` is true. Then `strcspn` is used to find the end of the line. But `strcspn` stops at null bytes. If the input has embedded null bytes, `strcspn` stops at the first null byte. The code then checks `CH(off) == _T('\0')` and increments `off`. But what if `off` reaches `ctx->size`? Then `CH(off)` reads `ctx->text[ctx->size]` which is out of bounds.

But wait - the input from the description ends with `6`, not a newline. So `doc_ends_with_newline` would be false. Unless the fuzz target's input is different.

Actually, let me re-read the description input more carefully:
```
